"I wrote the slice-4 plan to /tmp/claude-1000/-home-zhengyew-telelean/20626c17-0334-4498-ad86-9dc24b7010ed/scratchpad/plans/slice4-plan.md. It is 753 lines and covers slice 4 only. A separate agent is writing slice5-plan.md in the same folder; I only read it. I created no other file: two temporary check files I made by mistake in the scratchpad were deleted right away.\n\nThe plan follows the section layout of the slice-2 and slice-3 plans (§0–§9). The coverage table in §9 lists all 73 slice-4 audit rows, checked by script. It also lists G4, G5, G6, D2-13, D2-15, D3-1, D3-43's fallback, D3-49, the slice-4 parts of rows mapped elsewhere, and the four rows that need references first.\n\n**Core mechanism.** A transform is stored as a draft. One pure function (`analyseTransform`) analyses it, and one pure planner (`planTypedWrite`) writes it.\n- **Outside a session**, opening the window starts a real provisional session (new phase `transform`), because the user's words have the button create the session. If no Claude is needed, the session ends and the host writes the change as one board action, `apply-transform`.\n- **Inside a session**, each transform is one queue step at Submit review. The step starts from the judged head before telelean writes what you typed, so it is all or nothing.\n- **Ownership.** What you typed goes into a ledger `typed` entry. It is owned by \"typed\" items, which are accepted as yours once their dependencies are accepted.\n\n**Sub-slices**\n- **4a** (core): contracts, texts, `draftFromModel`, TR-12 (a), validators.\n- **4b** (core): the analysis — outcome, TR-13 dependents, TR-11 kinds with G6, session-target rules.\n- **4c** (core): the typed write, and the direct path's `apply-transform` plan.\n- **4d** (core): typed items — ownership, classes, dependencies, revert, the queue-end reconcile, backstop.\n- **4e** (core): judging transform steps (`no-proofs`, tool branches), session-start freezes, the transform reason, revalue.\n- **4f** (core): reducer (queue, replace, drop), `planSubmit` (transform and rework steps, cascade, `reworkResults` take-out), the Formalize check, the session view; the host answers `not-yet` until 4i.\n- **4g** (runner): TR-12 (b) scratch elaboration at the model root. Can run beside 4b–4f.\n- **4h** (runner): the transform step and Submit review with transforms.\n- **4i** (runner and host): the provisional session, windows in a stage, the direct path, TW-1 refusals, consent.\n- **4j** (webview): store, draft model, autosave and restore, entry gates.\n- **4k** (webview): the window itself.\n- **4l** (webview): entry points, review integration, session bar, end confirmation, user guide.\n- **4m**: Playwright specs, run by the integrator only.\n- **4x** (conditional): Formalize's \"Claude may add cards\", only if D3-43's fallback moved it here.\n\n**Decisions**\n- **D4-1** What you typed is recorded in a ledger `typed` entry and owned unit by unit by `typed:<card>` items, accepted as yours. Content copied from Claude stays in Claude's items.\n- **D4-2** With no session, opening the window starts a real provisional session (phase `transform`). There is no Claude, consent or Lean until needed, and Cancel is Exit.\n- **D4-3** A transform needing no Claude ends the provisional session, and the host then writes one board action, `apply-transform`. If a hand edit lands in that instant, the write is refused and the draft is lost (the toast says so).\n- **D4-4** One queue step per transform, starting from the judged head before the typed write. All or nothing: a step that fails is taken back whole and its transform stays queued with the reason.\n- **D4-5** A Submit review that carries out transforms always seals the stack and opens a new stage; it never goes straight to the end confirmation (narrows RV-12 and D2-54).\n- **D4-6** TR-12 (b) is checked against your files at the model root. Names that only visible session content defines are checked when the transform is carried out. Typed vocabulary declarations must elaborate now (narrows TR-12 (c) for declarations).\n- **D4-7** telelean writes every typed statement itself, including those naming undefined things; Claude only defines the names. No `typed-text` rule is needed.\n- **D4-8** Decline → Transform declines its items only when the transform is queued (Shaky-like). While queued, those items change only by dropping or editing the transform.\n- **D4-9** Values copied from Claude's proposals start open and marked \"Claude's proposal\" (refines TR-4).\n- **D4-10** The window's field states limit only that transform. The window's Context Menu freeze actions are real session freezes and persist (FROZEN 3). Typed fields get the `transform` reason.\n- **D4-11** \"Make it pending\" is the board's existing Mark pending, after Apply (narrows TR-14 (d)).\n- **D4-12** D2-15 is kept for every Shaky rework, transform-made items included: no splits. You split cards yourself through Decline → Transform.\n- **D4-13** No D-11 rewrite and no \"Delete anyway\" inside a session; both exist only for transforms applied directly.\n- **D4-14** A suggested premise can be transformed on its own: only its draft is edited, it never needs Claude, and it is checked again, then accepted with \"how you hold it\".\n- **D4-15** A new card whose sentence is left to Claude is a slot, created by `add_card`. Its other fields stay fuzzy, and no typed arrow touches it.\n- **D4-16** A direct transform freezes the typed fields of existing cards as standing freezes, as board edits do (D3-44). The provisional session's own freezes are dropped.\n- **D4-17** Session-start freezes cover every card and vocabulary item outside the window, whole. Unfreeze lifts a key for the session; the end confirmation sums them up in one line, with no keep rows.\n- **D4-18** The window is modal, with no Minimize, and opens only from the Proposed view or the review list. While it is open, actions that change the working copy, an item's status, or start Claude are refused. Esc never closes it.\n- **D4-19** For session targets, Kind, Dependencies and Proof are locked. Neither a target nor a card with an arrow into it can be trashed. A target's statement may change only when your files have no proof of it, and its Result is then reworked in the same queue.\n- **D4-20** Changing the Lean statement of a card whose proof is kept is refused: clear the proof.\n- **D4-21** In a session, a dependent can be left to Claude only if nothing but accepted items freezes it. A proof left to Claude becomes a Proof broken item. Frozen dependents must be unfrozen or deleted too.\n- **D4-22** Typed items need no own Accept, never keep the results hidden, and are untouched by D2-23. They can be rejected or transformed, not declined as Shaky; a cascade withdraws them.\n- **D4-23** A typed field's freeze compares text during its queue and its full value from the queue end. It replaces the expectations of the key's other non-accepted reasons (FZ-3 2e's revalue).\n- **D4-24** Shaky or to-be-redone items made by a transform step are reworked by one rework step per transform.\n- **D4-25** Transform-step tool set: no `freeze_statements`, `propose_assumption` or `delete_card`. It writes no proofs, changes no kinds, and finishes \"done\" or \"cannot\".\n- **D4-26** Transform is offered on Formalize, Change, Add, Delete, vocabulary-term, declaration, typed and (alone) Add premise items; not on Results, arrows or Proof broken. Ask me instead falls back to Shaky when none of its items takes Transform.\n- **D4-27** A transform-started session asks egress consent only before its first Claude step.\n- **D4-28** Slice 4 is built before slice 5 (AP-2), with small stubs slice 5 extends. The slice-5 plan's D5-1 agrees.\n- **D4-29** The typed write never partially edits Claude's content, so a typed sentence leaves the docstring mirror of Claude's statement unchanged (a cosmetic leftover).\n- **D4-30** A queued transform that changed since queueing \"needs a new look\" at Submit review. A card held by a queued transform opens in no other window.\n\n**Unresolved, for the integrator**\n- The slice-5 plan's §2.6 mentions slice 4's `revalued.held`. This plan has no held revalue: under D4-21, things that hold a changed name are unfrozen, deleted or cascaded. §3.12 explains this and names the single-call hooks slice 5 asked for; the slice-5 plan should match.\n- Slice 3 is not built yet, and slice 2 is still being built. Their actual contracts (freeze deltas, the `card` ledger entry, tool validators, `SessionPhase` switches) may differ; builders record any difference in §8.\n- Two assumptions for builders to confirm:\n  - **4g:** that Lean's \"unknown identifier\" messages attribute cleanly by line in the scratch file.\n  - **4i:** that the runner can read the board's model and facts as B in phase `transform` without a build.\n- 4x is built only if D3-43's fallback triggers in slice 3."